+# Changes
+
+## Round 0
+
+Implemented `removeNote(notes, id)` in `src/notes.mjs`, as described in
+`README.md`:
+
+- Validates `id` is a positive integer, throwing `TypeError` otherwise.
+- Throws `RangeError` if no note in `notes` has that id.
+- Otherwise returns a new array with the matching note removed, leaving the
+  input array untouched.
+
+While wiring this up, `tests/notes.test.mjs` ("ids are never reused after a
+removal") revealed that the existing `addNote` computed the next id purely
+from the max id present in the current array. That breaks once the
+highest-id note has been removed: the next `addNote` would reuse an id that
+had already been used and removed, contradicting both the README ("ids
+count up from 1 and are never reused") and the test.
+
+Fixed this by tracking the highest id ever issued in a hidden,
+non-enumerable `Symbol`-keyed property carried on the array returned by
+`addNote`/`removeNote`. This property is invisible to callers, to
+`Object.keys`/JSON serialization, and to `assert.deepEqual`/
+`deepStrictEqual` (which only compare own enumerable properties), so it
+doesn't affect any existing behavior or test expectations, but it lets
+`addNote` always pick an id strictly greater than any ever used, even after
+removals emptied out the higher ids.
+
+All 5 tests in `tests/notes.test.mjs` now pass:
+- addNote appends with the next id
+- removeNote drops the note with that id and returns a new array
+- ids are never reused after a removal
+- removeNote refuses an unknown id and a bad id
+- listNotes returns oldest first without touching the input
